%S A397572 1,1,275,164389,3580723,702220149049,3302111508005467,1310659481875,
%T A397572 130058831534712477399217,1709695157235335152669681981,
%U A397572 75414945726026671969,3510215157872449235583246414925322167,522173026970270010408214569091796875,2043114202677428031571574209385167
%N A397572 Absolute value of the field norm N_{Q(zeta_k)/Q}(S_k), where S_k is the half-period truncation at a primitive k-th root of unity of the parity-shifted q-series Sum_{r>=0} q^(r^2)/(q;-q)_r^2, and k = 4*n - 2.
%C A397572 Let F(q) = Sum_{r>=0} q^(r^2)/(q;-q)_r^2, where (q;-q)_r = Product_{j=1..r} (1 + (-q)^j). This is the parity-shifted companion of Ramanujan's third-order mock theta function f(q) = Sum_{r>=0} q^(r^2)/(-q;q)_r^2 (A000025); it is not one of Ramanujan's mock theta functions.
%C A397572 k == 2 (mod 4), put s = k/2 (odd), and let z = zeta_k. Then -z has odd order s, so no factor 1 + (-z)^j vanishes and every summand t_r = z^(r^2)/(z;-z)_r^2 of F(z) is defined. Also Product_{j=1..s} (1 + (-z)^j) = 2, as one sees by putting x = -1 in x^s - 1 = Product_{j=0..s-1} (x - (-z)^j); hence (z;-z)_{r+s} = 2*(z;-z)_r. And z^((r+s)^2) = -z^(r^2), because 2*r*s == 0 (mod k), s^2 == s (mod k) for odd s, and z^s = -1. So t_{r+s} = -t_r/4, the series converges at z, and
%C A397572 F(z) = (4/5) * Sum_{r=0..s-1} t_r = (16/15) * Sum_{r=0..k-1} t_r.
%C A397572 Here a(n) = abs(N(S_k)) for the half-period truncation S_k = Sum_{r=0..k/2-1} t_r, so that F(zeta_k) = (4/5)*S_k. Equivalently S_k = (4/3)*Sum_{r=0..k-1} t_r; the two expressions are identically equal, since the second half-block is exactly -1/4 times the first. Note that S_k is a normalized truncation and not the value of the series.
%C A397572 S_k lies in Z[zeta_k], so a(n) is a rational integer: for 1 <= j < s the factor 1 + (-z)^j = (1 - (-z)^(2j))/(1 - (-z)^j) is a cyclotomic unit, because -z has odd order s. Hence every denominator (z;-z)_r with r < s is a unit; the factor 2 first appears at j = s, just outside the truncation.
%C A397572 The collapse is special to k == 2 (mod 4). For k == 0 (mod 4) the factor 1 + (-z)^j vanishes at j = k/2, and for odd k it vanishes at j = k; in both cases the series has no value at zeta_k.
%C A397572 Empirical observations, verified by exact computation for 1 <= n <= 16:
%C A397572 (i) Every rational prime p dividing a(n), with p not dividing k, satisfies p == 1 (mod k); equivalently, p splits completely in Q(zeta_k).
%C A397572 (ii) a(n) is odd, and squarefree apart from primes dividing k. The only repeated prime divisor in this range is the ramified prime 5, at k = 10, 30, 50:
%C A397572 a(3) = 275 = 5^2 * 11,
%C A397572 a(8) = 1310659481875 = 5^4 * 31 * 571 * 118471,
%C A397572 a(13) = 522173026970270010408214569091796875 = 5^18 * 151 * 751 * 1151 * 1520801 * 689588101.
%C A397572 This is not a general pattern: at n = 21 (k = 82) the prime 83 divides a(21) exactly twice, although 83 does not divide 82. Observation (i) is unaffected, since 83 == 1 (mod 82).
%C A397572 (iii) a(n) == 1 (mod k) except at k = 10, 30, 50, where a(n) == 5, 25, 25 (mod k), respectively.
%C A397572 The terms were computed by exact arithmetic in Q(zeta_k), not by numerical approximation.
%H A397572 Vaclav Kotesovec, <a href="/A397572/b397572.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..100</a>
%H A397572 Kathrin Bringmann and Larry Rolen, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s40687-015-0035-8">Radial limits of mock theta functions</a>, Research in the Mathematical Sciences, Vol. 2, No. 1 (2015), Article 17.
%H A397572 Amanda Folsom, Ken Ono, and Robert C. Rhoades, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1017/fmp.2013.3">Mock theta functions and quantum modular forms</a>, Forum of Mathematics, Pi, Vol. 1, Cambridge University Press (2013), e2.
%F A397572 Let k = 4*n - 2, zeta = exp(2*Pi*i/k), and (zeta;-zeta)_r = Product_{j=1..r} (1 + (-zeta)^j). Then a(n) = abs(Norm_{Q(zeta_k)/Q}(S_k)), where
%F A397572 S_k = Sum_{r=0..k/2-1} zeta^(r^2)/(zeta;-zeta)_r^2 = (4/3) * Sum_{r=0..k-1}
%F A397572 zeta^(r^2)/(zeta;-zeta)_r^2.
%F A397572 Equivalently, let P_k(x) be the unique polynomial of degree < phi(k) representing S_k modulo the k-th cyclotomic polynomial Phi_k(x), with denominators inverted modulo Phi_k(x). Then a(n) = abs(Resultant(Phi_k(x), P_k(x))).
%e A397572 For n = 1, k = 2: S_2 = 1, so a(1) = 1.
%e A397572 For n = 3, k = 10: S_10 = 2*zeta^3 - zeta^2 - 3*zeta in Z[zeta_10], whose norm has absolute value 275 = 5^2 * 11, with 11 == 1 (mod 10) and 5 | 10 ramified.
%e A397572 For n = 4, k = 14: S_14 = 2*zeta^5 - 3*zeta^4 + 7*zeta^3 - 6*zeta^2 - zeta - 3 in Z[zeta_14], whose norm has absolute value 164389 = 43 * 3823, with 43 == 1 and 3823 == 1 (mod 14).
%o A397572 (PARI) a(n) = {my(k=4*n-2, f=polcyclo(k), z=Mod(x,f), S=Mod(0,f), P=Mod(1,f)); for(r=0, k/2-1, S += z^(r*r)/P^2; P *= 1 + (-z)^(r+1)); abs(norm(S));}
%o A397572 (SageMath)
%o A397572 def a(n):
%o A397572     k = 4*n - 2                    # k == 2 (mod 4)
%o A397572     K = CyclotomicField(k)
%o A397572     z = K.gen()
%o A397572     S = K(0)
%o A397572     poch = K(1)                    # (z;-z)_r, starting at r = 0
%o A397572     for r in range(k // 2):        # half a period suffices
%o A397572         S += z^(r*r) / poch^2
%o A397572         poch *= 1 + (-z)^(r+1)     # advance to (z;-z)_{r+1}
%o A397572     return ZZ(abs(S.norm(QQ)))
%o A397572 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 17)])
%Y A397572 Cf. A000025, A016825, A393567, A394831, A395116, A395117, A395950.
